-// How many pixels the guest is told it has per point.
-//
-// A macOS client asks the screen for its backing scale and computes every pixel
-// size from it - window backing store, drawable size, glyph sizes. On the iPad the
-// panel is 2732x2048 at a 2.0 scale, so a guest that is told 2.0 draws 5.6 million
-// pixels every frame, which is what a fanless M1 cannot sustain.
-//
-// Telling it 1.0 instead keeps every one of those calculations in the same units -
-// the guest still lays out 1366x1024 points, but each point is one pixel - so its
-// own coordinates, hit testing and pointer maths agree with what it draws, and
-// CoreAnimation stretches the 1366x1024 result to the panel with an exact 2x. This
-// is the documented way round: drawableSize is defined as bounds x contentsScale,
-// and the scale is the layer's, not something to be forged underneath it.
